menu "Parking Lot Camera"

    config DISPLAY_ENABLE
        bool "Enable the on-device status display (ST7789 240x240)"
        default y
        help
            Turn this off when the TFT is not fitted.

            The panel costs internal RAM (a 7.5 KB DMA bounce buffer per flush,
            plus the SPI bus and driver state) and real time in the display
            task. With the screen absent that is pure waste, and internal RAM is
            the scarcest resource on this board.

            With this off, display_init() returns immediately: no frame buffer is
            allocated, the SPI bus and the panel driver are never initialised, no
            display task is created, and every display_show_*() call becomes a
            no-op because there is no queue to post to. The camera node is
            unaffected.

    config WIFI_MAX_TX_POWER_DBM
        int "Wi-Fi maximum transmit power (dBm)"
        range 2 20
        default 10
        help
            Cap on the Wi-Fi transmit power, in dBm. 10 by default, not the 20 the
            hardware allows, and that is deliberate.

            This board's 3.3 V rail cannot carry the current spikes of a 20 dBm
            transmit burst. The evidence is a hardware comparison, not a guess: with
            the identical firmware, the stream delivers ~185 KB/s when the LCD module
            is plugged in and ~0.6 KB/s when it is not. The LCD is acting as extra
            decoupling capacitance, which is exactly what a sagging rail needs.

            A weak rail hits the uplink only, because receiving is cheap and
            transmitting is what draws the current, and it shows up as every one of
            these at once:
              - the AP's signal reads strong (-33 dBm) while the board struggles to
                be heard
              - small pings get 0% loss but 66-154 ms round trips (retries)
              - a 1.2 KB HTTP page completes in 0.19 s, while 11.7 KB JPEG frames
                wedge the socket (send timed out, EAGAIN)
              - ping loss 15% before the modem-sleep fix, 0% after, throughput still
                wrong

            The real fix is electrical - a better supply, a short thick cable, or a
            bulk capacitor (470-1000 uF plus 100 nF) across the 3.3 V pins. This
            setting only reduces the size of the current spikes so the board keeps
            working in the meantime. Lower it further (8, or even 2) if the link is
            still unreliable; the access point is close enough that the link budget
            is not the limiting factor.

            The value actually in force is read back from the driver and printed in
            the diagnostics line as tx=<dBm>.

    config OTA_MANIFEST_URL
        string "OTA manifest URL"
        default "http://192.168.3.102:8000/manifest.json"
        help
            Full URL of the thin OTA server's manifest.json.

            THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE IN THE FIRMWARE THAT CONTAINS AN OTA SERVER
            ADDRESS. Do not add a second copy anywhere else: no source file,
            no sdkconfig.defaults, no README snippet that people copy from.
            tools/ota-server/run.sh prints the correct value for this machine
            when it starts.

            The address is baked in at build time, so moving the OTA server to a
            different host or network means rebuilding. That is accepted
            deliberately - the alternative would be a discovery mechanism, and
            the device pull model is meant to stay this thin.

            To make a mismatch easy to spot, the value is logged at startup
            ("ota: manifest URL = ...") and shown on the device status page.

            Plain http:// is the intended sandbox-LAN setting. The downloaded
            image is always SHA256-verified against the manifest before it is
            committed, so HTTP is not trusted blindly. An https:// URL also
            works and is validated against the ESP x509 root bundle.

endmenu
